Certainly! To set up a shared Nix cache in a flake-based NixOS configuration across multiple hosts, you'll want to:

1. Designate one host as the Nix cache server.
2. Configure all other hosts to use this cache server as a substituter.
3. Ensure that builds on any host upload their results to the cache server, making them available to all.

Here's a comprehensive example of how you can achieve this in your flake configuration:

---

### 1. Define the Nix cache server host

Suppose you have a host named `cache-host`. On this host, you'll run a Nix binary cache server.

### 2. Set up the cache server

On `cache-host`, you can run a simple Nix binary cache server using `nix-serve` or `nix-cache-server`. For simplicity, let's assume you're using `nix-serve`.

**Example system configuration snippet for `cache-host`:**

```nix
# In your flake's `nixosConfigurations.cache-host`:
{
  imports = [ <nixpkgs/nixos/modules/services/misc/nix-serve.nix> ];

  services.nix-serve = {
    enable = true;
    port = 8080; # or any port you prefer
    # Optional: specify cache directory
    cacheDir = "/var/nix/cache";
  };

  # Ensure the cache directory exists
  system.activationScripts = {
    createCacheDir = ''
      mkdir -p /var/nix/cache
    '';
  };
}
```

Start and enable the service:

```bash
nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#cache-host
```

### 3. Configure other hosts to use the cache server

On all other hosts, you need to:

- Add the cache server as a substituter.
- Enable auto-upload of build results to the cache server.

**In your flake, for each client host:**

```nix
{
  # Example host configuration
  nix = {
    # Add the cache server as a substituter
    package = pkgs.nix;
    extraOptions = ''
      substituters = https://cache-host.example.com http://cache-host:8080 ${builtins.concatStringsSep " " (builtins.attrValues nix.binaryCaches)} ;
      trusted-substituters = https://cache-host.example.com http://cache-host:8080
      # Enable auto-upload
      trusted-public-keys = cache-host.example.com-1:YOUR_PUBLIC_KEY
    '';

    # Alternatively, set these options in `nix.conf`:
    nix.conf = ''
      substituters = https://cache-host.example.com http://cache-host:8080
      trusted-substituters = https://cache-host.example.com
      # Enable auto-upload
      experimental-features = nix-command flakes
      auto-optimise-store = true
    '';
  };
}
```

**Note:** To enable auto-upload, you can set:

```nix
{
  nix = {
    # ...
    extraOptions = ''
      # other options
      experimental-features = nix-command flakes
      # Enable auto-upload
      store = /nix/store
      auto-optimise-store = true
    '';
  };
}
```

But more reliably, you can set the environment variable:

```nix
{
  environment.variables = {
    NIX_REMOTE = "ssh://root@cache-host";
  };
}
```

Or, in your `nix.conf`:

```ini
substituters = https://cache-host.example.com http://cache-host:8080
trusted-substituters = https://cache-host.example.com
trusted-public-keys = cache-host.example.com-1:YOUR_PUBLIC_KEY
auto-optimise-store = true
```

**Important:** To enable auto-upload of build results, ensure that:

- The build host has write access to the cache directory on the cache server.
- The cache server is configured to accept uploads (nix-serve supports this by default).

---

### 4. Automate cache uploads during builds

Nix automatically uploads build results to the configured cache if:

- The cache server is trusted.
- The build is performed with `nix` configured to do so (which is default when using `nix` with `substituters` and `trusted-substituters`).

### 5. Final notes

- Make sure the cache server is accessible from all hosts.
- Secure the cache server if needed (e.g., via HTTPS, authentication).
- You can also use more advanced cache servers like [nix-cache-server](https://github.com/nix-community/nix-cache-server) for production setups.

---

### Example Flake Snippet

Here's a simplified example of how your `flake.nix` might look:

```nix
{
  description = "Multi-host NixOS configuration with shared cache";

  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-23.05"; # or your preferred nixpkgs
  };

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs }:
    let
      system = "x86_64-linux";
      pkgs = nixpkgs.legacyPackages.${system};
    in {
      nixosConfigurations = {
        cache-host = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
          system = "x86_64-linux";
          modules = [
            ./configuration-cache-host.nix
          ];
        };
        host1 = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
          system = "x86_64-linux";
          modules = [
            ./configuration-host1.nix
          ];
        };
        # Add more hosts as needed
      };
    };
}
```

And in `configuration-cache-host.nix`:

```nix
{ config, pkgs, ... }:

{
  imports = [ <nixpkgs/nixos/modules/services/misc/nix-serve.nix> ];

  services.nix-serve = {
    enable = true;
    port = 8080;
    cacheDir = "/var/nix/cache";
  };

  system.activationScripts.createCacheDir = ''
    mkdir -p /var/nix/cache
  '';
}
```

In `configuration-host1.nix`:

```nix
{ config, pkgs, ... }:

{
  nix = {
    extraOptions = ''
      substituters = https://cache-host.example.com http://cache-host:8080
      trusted-substituters = https://cache-host.example.com
      # Enable auto-upload
      experimental-features = nix-command flakes
    '';
  };
}
```

---

### Summary

- **Designate a host as cache server** and run `nix-serve`.
- **Configure other hosts** to use the cache server as a substituter and trusted source.
- **Builds on any host** will automatically upload to the cache server, making packages available cluster-wide.

This setup ensures efficient sharing of build artifacts and reduces build times across your NixOS fleet.

---

