In Delphi 7, there isn't a direct property in the `project.dpr` file to set a default font for the entire project at compile time. However, you can set the default font for all forms in your project by doing the following:

### Method 1: Set the default font for all forms at runtime

You can add code in your `project.dpr` file to set the font for all forms when the application starts. For example:

```pascal
program YourProjectName;

uses
  Forms,
  Unit1, // your main form
  // add other forms here if needed
  SysUtils;

{$R *.res}

begin
  Application.Initialize;

  // Set default font for all forms
  Application.OnCreateForm := 
    procedure (Sender: TObject; var aForm: TCustomForm)
    begin
      aForm.Font.Name := 'Arial'; // set your desired font name
      aForm.Font.Size := 10;      // set your desired font size
    end;

  Application.CreateForm(TForm1, Form1);
  Application.Run;
end.
```

**Note:** The `Application.OnCreateForm` event is available in Delphi 7, allowing you to customize each form as it is created.

### Method 2: Create a base form class

Alternatively, create a custom base form class that sets the font in its constructor, and then make all your forms inherit from this base class. This approach is cleaner and more maintainable.

1. Create a new unit, e.g., `BaseForm.pas`:

```pascal
unit BaseForm;

interface

uses
  Forms, Classes;

type
  TBaseForm = class(TForm)
  public
    constructor Create(AOwner: TComponent); override;
  end;

implementation

constructor TBaseForm.Create(AOwner: TComponent);
begin
  inherited Create(AOwner);
  Font.Name := 'Arial'; // your default font
  Font.Size := 10;      // your default size
end;

end.
```

2. Change your forms to inherit from `TBaseForm` instead of `TForm`.

### Summary

- You can't set a default font globally in `project.dpr` directly.
- Use `Application.OnCreateForm` to set the font at runtime for all forms.
- Or, create a base form class to enforce default font settings across your forms.

