Certainly! Here's a simple yet effective custom JavaScript router for a single-page application (SPA). It leverages modern JavaScript features like `history.pushState`, `popstate` event, and `async/await` for flexibility.

This router will:

- Map URL paths to handler functions or components.
- Handle navigation without page reloads.
- Support dynamic route matching (e.g., `/user/:id`).

---

### Basic Custom Router Implementation

```javascript
class Router {
  constructor() {
    this.routes = [];
    this._loadInitialRoute();
    window.addEventListener('popstate', () => this._loadRoute());
  }

  // Register a route with a path and a handler function
  addRoute(path, handler) {
    const routeRegex = this._pathToRegex(path);
    const paramNames = this._getParamNames(path);
    this.routes.push({ path, handler, routeRegex, paramNames });
  }

  // Navigate to a specific path programmatically
  navigate(path) {
    history.pushState({}, '', path);
    this._loadRoute();
  }

  // Convert route path to regex for matching
  _pathToRegex(path) {
    const regexString = '^' + path.replace(/:\w+/g, '([^/]+)') + '$';
    return new RegExp(regexString);
  }

  // Extract parameter names from route path
  _getParamNames(path) {
    const paramNames = [];
    const paramRegex = /:(\w+)/g;
    let match;
    while ((match = paramRegex.exec(path)) !== null) {
      paramNames.push(match[1]);
    }
    return paramNames;
  }

  // Match the current URL to a route
  _matchRoute(pathname) {
    for (const route of this.routes) {
      const match = pathname.match(route.routeRegex);
      if (match) {
        const params = {};
        route.paramNames.forEach((name, index) => {
          params[name] = match[index + 1];
        });
        return { route, params };
      }
    }
    return null;
  }

  // Load the route based on current URL
  async _loadRoute() {
    const pathname = window.location.pathname;
    const match = this._matchRoute(pathname);
    if (match) {
      const { route, params } = match;
      await route.handler({ params });
    } else {
      // Optional: handle 404
      console.error('No route matched.');
    }
  }

  // Load initial route on page load
  _loadInitialRoute() {
    this._loadRoute();
  }
}
```

---

### Usage Example

Suppose you have an HTML structure like:

```html
<div id="app"></div>
```

And you want to define routes:

```javascript
const router = new Router();

router.addRoute('/', async () => {
  document.getElementById('app').innerHTML = '<h1>Home Page</h1><a href="/about" data-link>About</a>';
});

router.addRoute('/about', async () => {
  document.getElementById('app').innerHTML = '<h1>About Page</h1><a href="/" data-link>Home</a>';
});

router.addRoute('/user/:id', async ({ params }) => {
  document.getElementById('app').innerHTML = `<h1>User Profile for ID: ${params.id}</h1><a href="/" data-link>Home</a>`;
});

// Intercept link clicks for SPA navigation
document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  const target = e.target.closest('a');
  if (target && target.matches('a[data-link]')) {
    e.preventDefault();
    const href = target.getAttribute('href');
    router.navigate(href);
  }
});
```

---

### Summary

- The `Router` class manages route registration, matching, and navigation.
- Routes can include dynamic segments (e.g., `:id`).
- Navigation is handled via `history.pushState` and `popstate`.
- Link clicks are intercepted to prevent full page reloads.

---

### Enhancements (Optional)

- Add support for query parameters.
- Implement a 404 route.
- Support nested routes.
- Lazy load components or content.

Feel free to customize and extend this basic router to fit your application's needs!

